# Videos 16–60: complete DRAFT packages (shorter). Same fields; refine VO/storyboard before production.
# (n, pillar, audience, title, hook, voiceover, demo_feature_or_None)
D = [
(16,"Risk management","Beginners","The 1% rule, honestly","Risking 1% per trade sounds boring. That's the point.","Risking one percent per trade sounds boring. That's the point. At one percent, ten losses in a row costs about ten percent of your account — painful, survivable. At ten percent per trade, the same streak can wipe you out. Losing streaks happen to every strategy. Your risk per trade decides whether you're still around when the good trades come. Write your risk in dollars on every trade before you enter.",None),
(17,"Trading misconceptions","Social-media influenced beginners","Screenshots of wins aren't a track record","A screenshot of a win proves one trade happened.","A screenshot of a winning trade proves one thing: one trade happened. It doesn't show the losses before it, the size, or the account. A track record is every trade, wins and losses, over months. Before you copy anyone — including yourself — ask to see the full list. That's what a journal is.",None),
(18,"Performance analysis","Traders with 30+ trades","Profit factor in plain English","Profit factor 1.5. Good or bad?","Profit factor is your total winnings divided by your total losses. One point five means for every dollar lost, you made a dollar fifty. Below one means you're losing overall. But with fewer than thirty trades, it can swing wildly — two lucky trades can make anyone look great. That's why TradeNotes warns you when your sample is small.","Dashboard profit factor + small-sample warning"),
(19,"Reviewing losing trades","Intermediate","Your biggest loss deserves 10 minutes","Your biggest loss this month is your best teacher.","Take your single biggest loss this month and give it ten minutes. What was the plan? Was the size normal? Did you move the stop? Was it in your best session or your worst? Most big losses aren't bad luck — they're a normal mistake on a bigger size. Find which one.","Analytics 'worst trade'"),
(20,"Discipline","Beginners","A trading plan on one index card","If your trading plan doesn't fit on a card, you won't follow it.","If your trading plan doesn't fit on one index card, you won't follow it in the moment. Write: what you trade, when, your setup in one sentence, where the stop goes, risk per trade, and when you stop for the day. Keep it next to your screen. Log the strategy name on every trade so you can see if the card works.",None),
(21,"Beginner trading education","Beginners","What a pip is","A pip is smaller than you think.","A pip is usually the fourth decimal place in a forex price — from one-oh-eight-five-oh to one-oh-eight-five-one is one pip. For yen pairs it's the second decimal. On one standard lot of EURUSD a pip is about ten dollars. Know your pip value before you know your stop size.",None),
(22,"TradeNotes demonstrations","New users","Your first 3 trades in TradeNotes","Here's what to do in your first 5 minutes.","New to TradeNotes? Do three things. Log your last trade — even if it was weeks ago. Add a stop so you get R. Tag one mistake if there was one. Then check the dashboard — every number has an explanation. Sample data shown.","Onboarding + first trade"),
(23,"Trading psychology","Beginners","FOMO entries","The candle's already gone. You buy anyway.","The candle has already run, everyone's posting about it, and you buy anyway. That's FOMO, and you're usually buying from the people taking profit. Tag FOMO trades for a month and compare their average R to your planned trades. Let the data talk you out of it.","Emotion tag analytics"),
(24,"Better trading habits","All","Pre-trade checklist","Three questions before you click buy.","Before you click buy: Is this my setup? Where am I wrong? How much am I risking in dollars? If you can't answer all three in ten seconds, don't take it. Write the answer in your pre-trade note.",None),
(25,"Trading mistakes","Beginners","Oversizing after a win","A big win can be more dangerous than a loss.","After a big win you feel invincible, so the next trade is bigger. Then a normal loss wipes out the win and more. Keep size tied to risk percent, not mood. Tag 'Oversized' when you break it.",None),
(26,"Performance analysis","Intermediate","Average win vs average loss","Two numbers that explain your account.","If your average win is smaller than your average loss, you need a high win rate just to break even. Look at both numbers together, not separately. Then decide which one to work on: cutting losers faster or letting winners run.","Dashboard averages"),
(27,"Journaling","Beginners","Write the 'why' before the result","Write why you entered before you know how it ends.","Write your reason for the trade before you know the result. Afterwards, your brain rewrites the story to fit the outcome. A pre-trade note is the only honest record of what you were thinking.","Pre-trade notes field"),
(28,"Trading misconceptions","Beginners","'More trades = more money'","Trading more isn't working harder.","More trades doesn't mean more money. It usually means more fees, more mediocre setups and more mistakes. Compare your days with one or two trades to your days with ten. Many traders find the busy days are the losing days.",None),
(29,"Risk management","Beginners","Reward-to-risk before entry","Know your target before you enter.","Before entry, divide the distance to your target by the distance to your stop. Two to one means you can be wrong more often than right and still be fine — if you actually hold to target. TradeNotes shows reward-to-risk as you type.","Live reward:risk read-out"),
(30,"Discipline","All","The daily loss limit","Decide when you stop before you start.","Set a daily loss limit — say two R. Hit it, you're done for the day. No exceptions. Most blown accounts come from one bad day that turned into a terrible one.",None),
(31,"Reviewing losing trades","All","Losses with no notes","The losses you didn't write about are the ones repeating.","Scroll back through your journal and find the losses with no notes. Those are the trades you didn't want to think about — and usually the ones that keep happening. Write one line on each.",None),
(32,"TradeNotes demonstrations","Existing users","Mistake-tag breakdown","Which mistake costs you the most?","Every mistake tag in TradeNotes gets its own row in analytics: how many trades, net result, average R. Sample data here shows 'Moved stop' costing more than everything else combined. Find yours.","Analytics by mistake tag"),
(33,"Beginner trading education","Beginners","Spread and fees are a cost","You start every trade slightly losing.","Every trade starts slightly negative because of spread and commission. On small targets that cost is a big percentage. Log fees on every trade so your results are real.","Fees field"),
(34,"Trading psychology","Intermediate","Fear of pulling the trigger","Missing good setups is also a mistake.","Hesitating and missing your setup is a mistake too — it just doesn't show in your P and L. Tag 'Hesitant' when you enter late or skip. The pattern tells you whether your rules are unclear or your size is too big to feel comfortable.","Emotion tag 'Hesitant'"),
(35,"Better trading habits","All","Trading journal = feedback loop","Practice without feedback is just repetition.","Practice without feedback is just repetition. Trading gives feedback in money, but it's noisy. A journal turns it into something you can learn from: what you did, why, and what happened.",None),
(36,"Performance analysis","Intermediate","Weekday breakdown","Is Friday your worst day?","Group trades by weekday. Some traders find one day — often Monday or Friday — consistently negative. Small sample? Keep logging. Clear pattern? Trade less that day.","By weekday breakdown"),
(37,"Trading mistakes","Beginners","Closing winners too early","Cutting winners short is a mistake too.","Taking profit at the first wobble feels safe, but it caps your average win. Tag 'Closed too early' and check how far price went after. Then decide on a rule.",None),
(38,"Journaling","All","Rate your discipline 1–5","One number that predicts your results.","After each trade, rate your discipline from one to five. After a month, compare results by score. For most people, the fives make money and the ones don't — whatever the market did.","Discipline score"),
(39,"Trading misconceptions","Beginners","'Backtesting proves it works'","A backtest is a hypothesis, not a promise.","A backtest shows how rules would have done on past data — without your emotions, slippage or hesitation. It's a starting point, not proof. Treat results as hypothetical and confirm with a live journal.",None),
(40,"Risk management","Beginners","Position size from your stop","Your stop decides your size. Not the other way around.","Pick your stop where the setup is wrong. Then size the position so that distance equals your risk amount. Bigger stop, smaller size. That's how risk stays constant.",None),
(41,"Discipline","All","Rules you broke this week","Count your rule breaks, not your losses.","At the end of the week, count trades tagged 'Broke rules'. That number is more within your control than your P and L. Aim to make it smaller each week.",None),
(42,"TradeNotes demonstrations","Paid-curious users","Export your trades","Your data should never be locked in.","In TradeNotes, Settings, Your data, download every trade as a spreadsheet — notes, tags, exits, all of it. Free plan included. Your journal is yours.","CSV export"),
(43,"Beginner trading education","Beginners","Long vs short","You can profit when price falls — and lose when it rises.","Going long means you profit if price rises. Going short means you profit if it falls — and lose if it rises. Same risk rules apply both ways. Log the side correctly, or every number is backwards.",None),
(44,"Trading psychology","All","Overconfidence after a streak","Five wins in a row is when you're most dangerous.","After a winning streak, rules feel optional. That's when size creeps up and setups get looser. Check your discipline scores after streaks.",None),
(45,"Better trading habits","All","Stop when you're tilted","Tilt isn't a strategy.","If you notice you're angry, rushing or chasing, stop. Close the platform. Log what happened while it's fresh. Tomorrow-you will thank you.",None),
(46,"Performance analysis","Intermediate","Equity curve shapes","Your equity curve tells a story.","A steady upward line, a jagged staircase, or a cliff — your equity curve shows consistency, not just profit. A few big wins carrying lots of small losses is fragile.","Equity curve"),
(47,"Trading mistakes","Beginners","No plan for the exit","Entries get all the attention. Exits decide the result.","Most beginners plan the entry and improvise the exit. Decide your stop and target before you click — and note whether you followed them.",None),
(48,"Reviewing losing trades","Intermediate","Same mistake, different trade","It's not ten mistakes. It's one mistake ten times.","Review a month of losses and you'll often find one mistake repeated in different forms. Fix that one and many losses disappear.","Analytics by mistake tag"),
(49,"Journaling","All","Journal on mobile","Log the trade before you leave the desk.","The best time to log is right after the trade. TradeNotes works on your phone, so log it in a minute — even on the bus.","Mobile trade form"),
(50,"Trading misconceptions","Beginners","Indicators aren't strategies","An indicator isn't a reason.","An RSI reading isn't a strategy. A strategy says when you enter, where you're wrong, how much you risk and when you exit. Indicators are just inputs.",None),
(51,"Discipline","All","Same setup, every time","Consistency makes your data useful.","If every trade is a different idea, your journal can't tell you anything. Repeat a small number of setups and name them — then analytics can compare them.","Strategy breakdown"),
(52,"Risk management","All","Correlated trades","Three trades can be one big trade.","Buying EURUSD, GBPUSD and selling USDCHF at once is mostly one bet against the dollar. Add up the risk across correlated positions.",None),
(53,"Beginner trading education","Beginners","Demo vs live","Demo results don't include fear.","Demo trading teaches the buttons, not the emotions. Journal demo and live separately and compare discipline scores.",None),
(54,"TradeNotes demonstrations","All","Every metric explained","Not sure what a number means? Tap it.","Every statistic in TradeNotes links to a plain-English explanation and tells you when it's too early to trust. Sample data shown.","Learn links"),
(55,"Trading psychology","All","Detach from single trades","One trade means almost nothing.","Any single trade is mostly noise. Judge yourself over twenty or more. That's why the journal warns about small samples.","Small-sample warning"),
(56,"Better trading habits","All","Monthly review in 15 minutes","Once a month, zoom out.","Once a month: total R, best and worst setups, most common mistake, best session. Pick one change for next month.",None),
(57,"Performance analysis","Intermediate","Strategy comparison","Which of your setups actually works?","Name your setups and log them. After enough trades, compare them side by side. Many traders drop their favourite setup once they see the numbers.","By strategy"),
(58,"Trading mistakes","Beginners","Trading the news without a plan","News candles move fast in both directions.","Big news releases can spike price both ways in seconds, with wide spreads. If you trade news, have a specific plan — or step aside.",None),
(59,"Journaling","All","90-day journaling challenge","Log every trade for 90 days.","Challenge: log every trade for ninety days. Minimum fields only. At the end you'll know your best setup, worst habit and real risk per trade.",None),
(60,"TradeNotes demonstrations","All","What TradeNotes is (and isn't)","TradeNotes won't tell you what to trade.","TradeNotes won't tell you what to trade or promise profits. It records your trades, explains your numbers and shows your habits — so you can make better decisions yourself. Free to start.","Overview"),
]
